  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2018 and supported by European Research Council funding, <em>Virtual Works \u2013 Actual Things</em> has begun to register scholarly uptake, with 6 citations in OpenAlex. The citing record is still early, but it already shows the book being read across musicology, philosophy, and adjacent fields concerned with aesthetics, materiality, and interpretation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Paulo de Assis\u2019s <em>Virtual Works \u2013 Actual Things</em> contributes to debates about what musical works are and how they come into being, situating music ontology and performance within wider questions of aesthetics, epistemology, and art history. The work\u2019s impact profile is still modest in size, yet the citation record shows that its ideas have begun to travel beyond a single specialist niche.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of influence is scholarly citation. OpenAlex records 6 citations, with the citing activity spread from 2018 through 2023 and rising most visibly in 2023. Those citing works appear in venues including <em>Popular Music</em>, <em>twentieth-century music</em>, <em>Environment and Planning F</em>, and <em>Contributions to hermeneutics</em>, suggesting that the book has resonated not only in musicological discussions but also in interdisciplinary work on interpretation, material agency, and cultural theory.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The thematic range of the citing literature is similarly instructive. Most of the cited works cluster around musicology and musical analysis, as well as music technology and sound studies, but the surrounding concepts also reach into posthumanist ethics, urban governance, and visual culture. That pattern indicates a book whose conceptual vocabulary is proving useful in conversations about agency, embodiment, and the status of works across different scholarly contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows signs of broader circulation through library discovery and open access availability. WorldCat lists holdings under subjects such as theory of music and musicology, music philosophy and aesthetics, and ontology, and OpenAIRE identifies open instances of the book. While event and social-discourse data are not available here, the evidence that is present points to a work positioned for continued academic use, especially in research and teaching environments concerned with music\u2019s philosophical foundations.</p>",
